    <statement><p>Consider the ellipse
<me>\left(\frac{x-2}{8}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{y-9}{2}\right)^2 = 1</me>
Find the equation of the ellipse translated so that its center is at the origin.</p>
<p><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/><m>=1</m></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{x^{2}}{64}+\frac{y^{2}}{4}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} +  \frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1</me>
describes an ellipse with center <m>(h,k)</m>. 


Thus, for our ellipse to be located at the origin, it must have equation
<me>\frac{x^2}{{8}^2} +  \frac{y^2}{{2}^2} = 1</me>


</solution>
